# Normalize line endings. Rust/TOML/JSON source is LF on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Cargo.lock is generated by cargo, which writes LF itself.
Cargo.lock text eol=lf

# Batch files are the one exception to the blanket rule above: cmd.exe can misparse an
# LF-only script - labels and `goto` targets are the usual casualties - and it fails on
# double-click, not in the editor, so the place nobody looks is the place that broke.
# eol=crlf keeps the blob LF in the object store (a diff-clean repo on every platform)
# and writes CRLF only into the working copy.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Round-trip fixtures must be byte-exact. The do-no-harm rule (docs section 4.5) exists to
# prove we preserve encoding, BOM, line endings and trailing newline; a Git-managed EOL
# conversion would silently normalise the very bytes the tests assert on. Git must never
# touch them, in either direction.
crates/core/tests/fixtures/** -text -diff
crates/core/tests/fixtures/** binary

*.png binary
*.ico binary
